#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:1
msgid "Sphinx Quickstart Template"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:3
msgid ""
"This article is intended to take someone in the state of “I want to write "
"documentation and get it added to LLVM’s docs” and help them start writing "
"documentation as fast as possible and with as little nonsense as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:9
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:11
msgid ""
"LLVM documentation is written in [Markedly Structured Text (MyST)][myst] and "
"[reStructuredText (reST)][reStructuredText]. MyST is a Markdown flavor that "
"adds Sphinx documentation extensions. Markdown is preferred for new docs, "
"and migrating old docs from reStructuredText to Markdown is an open, ongoing "
"project. [Sphinx], a documentation generator originally written for Python "
"documentation, generates the LLVM HTML documentation from MyST and reST."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:16
msgid ""
"See the {ref}`migration <markdown_migration_guidelines>` section for more "
"information on how to migrate existing docs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:22
msgid "How to use this template"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:24
msgid ""
"This article is located in `docs/SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md`. To use it as "
"a template, make a copy and open it in a text editor. You can then write "
"your docs, and open a [GitHub PR](project:GitHub.rst) to request a review."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:28
msgid ""
"To view the Markdown source file for this article, click **Show Source** on "
"the right sidebar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:30
msgid "Authoring Guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:32
msgid ""
"Focus on *content*. It is easy to fix the Markdown syntax later if "
"necessary, and Markdown intentionally imitates common plain-text conventions "
"so it should be quite natural. A basic knowledge of Markdown syntax is "
"useful when writing the document, so the last ~half of this document "
"(starting with [Example Section](#example-section)) gives examples which "
"should cover 99% of use cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:39
msgid ""
"Let me say that again: focus on *content*. But if you really need to verify "
"Sphinx's output, see `docs/README.txt` for information on how to build it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:42
msgid "Creating New Articles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:44
msgid "Before creating a new article, consider the following questions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:46
msgid "Why would I want to read this document?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:48
msgid "What should I know to be able to follow along with this document?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:50
msgid "What will I have learned by the end of this document?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:52
msgid ""
"A standard best practice is to make your articles task-oriented. You "
"generally should not be writing documentation that isn't based around \"how "
"to\" do something unless there's already an existing \"how to\" article for "
"the topic you're documenting. The reason for this is that without a \"how "
"to\" article to read first, it might be difficult for someone unfamiliar "
"with the topic to understand a more advanced, conceptual article."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:56
msgid ""
"When creating a task-oriented article, follow existing LLVM articles by "
"giving it a filename that starts with `HowTo*.md`. This format is usually "
"the easiest for another person to understand and also the most useful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:58
msgid "Focus on content (yes, I had to say it again)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:60
msgid ""
"The rest of this document shows example Markdown and MyST markup constructs "
"that are meant to be read by you in your text editor after you have copied "
"this file into a new file for the documentation you are about to write."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:64
msgid "Example Section"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:66
msgid ""
"An article can contain one or more sections (i.e., headings). Sections (like "
"`Example Section` above) help give your document its structure. Use `#` for "
"the document title, `##` for top-level sections, `###` for subsections, and "
"so on. Leave a blank line before and after each heading."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:71
msgid "Example Nested Subsection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:73
msgid ""
"Subsections can also be nested beneath other subsections. For more "
"information on Markdown syntax, see the [CommonMark spec] and the [MyST "
"syntax guide]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:79
msgid "Text Formatting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:81
msgid "Text can be *emphasized*, **bold**, or `monospace`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:83
msgid "To create a new paragraph, simply insert a blank line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:85
msgid "Links"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SphinxQuickstartTemplate.md:87
msgid ""
"You can format a link [like this](https://llvm.org/). A more [sophisticated "
"syntax] allows you to place the `[link text]: <URL>` block pretty much "
"anywhere else in the document. This is useful when linking to especially "
"long URLs."
msgstr ""

